{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KMG46bDnppvFJvwTajcpLQ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I've found the parseArgs function and all related flag parsing. Here's what I found:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe ls implementation has comprehensive flag and option handling in three key functions:\n\n### 1. **parseArgs function** (lines 97-127)\nThis is the main entry point for parsing command-line arguments. It handles:\n- **Long options**:\n  - `--help` - displays help text and exits\n  - `--version` - displays version info and exits\n  - `--color` - enables color output (default mode is \"always\")\n  - `--color=WHEN` - accepts \"always\", \"auto\", or \"never\" as values\n  - `--` - stops option parsing, treats remaining args as paths\n  - Unrecognized long options starting with `--` produce an error and exit with code 2\n\n- **Short flags**: Delegated to parseShortFlags for multi-character options\n- **Paths**: Non-option arguments are collected as file paths\n\n### 2. **parseShortFlags function** (lines 129-205)\nHandles individual short flag characters:\n- `1` - single column listing\n- `a` - show all entries (including . and ..)\n- `A` - show almost all (exclude . and ..)\n- `C` - list by columns (default when output is terminal)\n- `d` - list directories as entries, not their contents\n- `F` - classify files (append type indicators: /=>@|*)\n- `h` - human-readable sizes (with -l and/or -s)\n- `i` - show inode numbers\n- `l` - long listing format\n- `n` - long format with numeric user/group IDs\n- `R` - recursive listing\n- `r` - reverse sort order\n- `s` - show block allocation\n- `S` - sort by file size (largest first)\n- `t` - sort by modification time (newest first)\n- `U` - no sorting (directory order)\n- `v` - natural version number sorting\n- `x` - list by horizontal lines\n- Invalid flags trigger an error and exit with code 2\n\n### 3. **parseColorValue function** (lines 207-220)\nValidates the `--color` argument, accepting only \"always\", \"auto\", or \"never\". Invalid values exit with code 2.\n\n### Key Implementation Details\n\n- The options are stored in a `struct options` (lines 54-74) with boolean and string fields\n- Flag combinations set/unset interdependent options (e.g., `-l` enables longFormat and disables other layout modes)\n- Error handling consistently outputs to stderr and exits with code 2 for argument errors\n- The `--` double dash stops option parsing and allows files starting with `-`\n\nThis is what you'll need to match in the vdir implementation to ensure compatibility."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"946f5fb5-d9d1-460a-9493-79b885905591","uuid":"4baca833-e844-4409-8fca-1a161f826950","timestamp":"2026-05-27T20:56:48.694Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the go-unix-utils repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5613, find cmd/ls/main.go and show me its parseArgs function and any long option handling. I need to see which flags and long options the ls implementation accepts so I can ensure vdir accepts the same ones. Show the full parseArgs function and any related flag parsing functions.","agentId":"a4cfe5fecc53fba49","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I've found the parseArgs function and all related flag parsing. Here's what I found:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe ls implementation has comprehensive flag and option handling in three key functions:\n\n### 1. **parseArgs function** (lines 97-127)\nThis is the main entry point for parsing command-line arguments. It handles:\n- **Long options**:\n  - `--help` - displays help text and exits\n  - `--version` - displays version info and exits\n  - `--color` - enables color output (default mode is \"always\")\n  - `--color=WHEN` - accepts \"always\", \"auto\", or \"never\" as values\n  - `--` - stops option parsing, treats remaining args as paths\n  - Unrecognized long options starting with `--` produce an error and exit with code 2\n\n- **Short flags**: Delegated to parseShortFlags for multi-character options\n- **Paths**: Non-option arguments are collected as file paths\n\n### 2. **parseShortFlags function** (lines 129-205)\nHandles individual short flag characters:\n- `1` - single column listing\n- `a` - show all entries (including . and ..)\n- `A` - show almost all (exclude . and ..)\n- `C` - list by columns (default when output is terminal)\n- `d` - list directories as entries, not their contents\n- `F` - classify files (append type indicators: /=>@|*)\n- `h` - human-readable sizes (with -l and/or -s)\n- `i` - show inode numbers\n- `l` - long listing format\n- `n` - long format with numeric user/group IDs\n- `R` - recursive listing\n- `r` - reverse sort order\n- `s` - show block allocation\n- `S` - sort by file size (largest first)\n- `t` - sort by modification time (newest first)\n- `U` - no sorting (directory order)\n- `v` - natural version number sorting\n- `x` - list by horizontal lines\n- Invalid flags trigger an error and exit with code 2\n\n### 3. **parseColorValue function** (lines 207-220)\nValidates the `--color` argument, accepting only \"always\", \"auto\", or \"never\". Invalid values exit with code 2.\n\n### Key Implementation Details\n\n- The options are stored in a `struct options` (lines 54-74) with boolean and string fields\n- Flag combinations set/unset interdependent options (e.g., `-l` enables longFormat and disables other layout modes)\n- Error handling consistently outputs to stderr and exits with code 2 for argument errors\n- The `--` double dash stops option parsing and allows files starting with `-`\n\nThis is what you'll need to match in the vdir implementation to ensure compatibility."}],"totalDurationMs":9464,"totalTokens":27144,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10375,"cache_read_input_tokens":16111,"output_tokens":653,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_r